---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import MadeByKaj from '../../../components/MadeByKaj.astro';
import LiveCodePlayground from '../../../components/live/LiveCodePlayground.astro';
import CodeBlockWithCopy from '../../../components/live/CodeBlockWithCopy.astro';
import createBrowserHtml from '../../../live-examples/skykit/create-a-browser/index.html?raw';

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const installCode = `npm install @found-in-space/skykit three`;
const importCode = `import * as THREE from 'three';
import {
  createSkykitViewer,
  createStreamingStarsPlugin,
  createKeyboardNavigationPlugin,
  createSkyGrabPlugin,
  createSkykitAnimationLoop,
} from '@found-in-space/skykit';
import {
  OCTREE_DEFAULT,
  createStarOctreeProviderService,
} from '@found-in-space/star-octree-provider';
import { createObserverShellStrategy } from '@found-in-space/star-trees';
import { createThreeStarField } from '@found-in-space/three-star-field';`;
---

<Layout
	title="Create a SkyKit browser | Found in Space"
	description="Create the smallest useful SkyKit star browser in a live editable browser example."
>
	<SiteHeader section="Learn & build" />

	<main class="page page--lesson">
		<header class="lesson-hero">
			<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/`}>Learn &amp; Build</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/`}>SkyKit</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<span>Create a browser</span>
			</nav>
			<p class="eyebrow">Lesson 1</p>
			<h1>Create a SkyKit browser</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Create the smallest useful SkyKit star browser: a host element, a Three.js renderer,
				a camera, a star provider, a star-field renderer, streaming stars, keyboard flight,
				drag-to-look controls, an animation loop, and cleanup.
			</p>
		</header>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section>
				<h2>Learning goal</h2>
				<p>
					SkyKit composes the browser experience. The star-octree provider streams cells,
					three-star-field draws those stars in Three.js, and the viewer coordinates the
					plugins that make the scene navigable.
				</p>
				<p>
					This first example starts from browser ESM imports so the first lesson is browser-first.
					No npm install, Vite project, or TypeScript setup is required to understand the shape.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>

		<LiveCodePlayground
			id="skykit-create-a-browser"
			title="Small SkyKit star browser"
			description="Edit the source, then run it. The preview streams stars in a sandboxed iframe."
			initialCode={createBrowserHtml}
			mode="html"
			height="34rem"
		/>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section class="callout panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Things to try</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Change <code>limitingMagnitude</code> from <code>6.5</code> to <code>7.5</code>.</li>
					<li>Change <code>speedPcPerSec</code> in the keyboard plugin.</li>
					<li>Change the star-field <code>exposure</code>.</li>
					<li>Move the initial <code>observerPc</code> away from the Sun.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Higher limiting magnitudes can stream more data and may be slower on some devices.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What changed in the code</h2>
				<p>
					The host element gives SkyKit somewhere to mount. The renderer and camera are ordinary
					Three.js objects. The provider reads the public render octree, while the streaming
					plugin asks for cells using <code>createObserverShellStrategy()</code>. Keyboard and
					grab plugins add control surfaces, and <code>createSkykitAnimationLoop()</code> keeps
					the viewer moving.
				</p>
				<p>
					The cleanup path matters. The example disposes the loop, viewer, and provider on
					<code>pagehide</code> and <code>beforeunload</code>, and the iframe is replaced before
					each rerun so previous WebGL resources and streams do not keep running.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="source-panel panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Source and next steps</p>
				<h2>Compare this to the package examples.</h2>
				<p>
					This live example is intentionally small. The package examples in the SkyKit repository
					show the same ideas with more diagnostics and local development support.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit" rel="noopener noreferrer">SkyKit repository</a></li>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit/tree/main/packages/skykit/examples/free-roam-lesson" rel="noopener noreferrer">Free-roam lesson example</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/add-an-object/`}>Next: add your own object to space</a></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="local-panel panel">
				<h2>Use this locally</h2>
				<p>
					When you move this into a local app, install the packages and use normal package
					imports instead of browser ESM URLs.
				</p>
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={installCode} label="Install" language="bash" />
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={importCode} label="Local imports" language="js" />
			</section>
		</article>

		<MadeByKaj />
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page--lesson {
		max-width: 76rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero {
		max-width: 60rem;
		padding: 3.5rem 0 1.5rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 4rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: 0;
	}

	.lesson-body {
		max-width: 54rem;
	}

	.source-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.source-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.local-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.local-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.lesson-hero h1 {
			font-size: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
